4. Time Quantum

**Time Quantum** is a concept in TLM2 (Transaction Level Modeling 2) that relates to temporal decoupling and the management of simulation time in loosely-timed models. Here are the key points:

* **Definition**:
  * A Time Quantum is the maximum amount of time that a process (typically an initiator) is allowed to run ahead of the global simulation time.

* **Purpose**:
  * It helps balance simulation efficiency with timing accuracy in loosely-timed models.
  * Allows processes to execute multiple transactions without constantly synchronizing with the global simulation time.

* **Temporal Decoupling**:
  * Time Quantum is a key component of temporal decoupling, where processes can run ahead of the global simulation time.
  * This improves simulation performance by reducing the number of context switches between processes.

* **Synchronization**:
  * When a process reaches its time quantum limit, it must synchronize with the global simulation time.
  * This ensures that all processes stay relatively close in simulated time, maintaining overall timing consistency.

* **Flexibility**:
  * The size of the time quantum can be adjusted based on the needs of the simulation.
  * Smaller quanta provide more accurate timing but may slow down simulation; larger quanta improve performance but may reduce timing accuracy.


```systemverilog
class my_initiator extends uvm_component;
  uvm_tlm_b_initiator_socket #(uvm_tlm_generic_payload) socket;
  time_quantum_manager tqm;

  function new(string name, uvm_component parent);
    super.new(name, parent);
    socket = new("socket", this);
    tqm = new("tqm");  // Create a time quantum manager
  endfunction

  task run_phase(uvm_phase phase);
    uvm_tlm_generic_payload tx;
    uvm_tlm_time delay = new("delay");
    time local_time = 0;  // Track local time within the time quantum

    // Set the time quantum to 100 ns
    tqm.set_global_quantum(100ns);

    phase.raise_objection(this);

    repeat(10) begin
      // Create and configure a new transaction
      tx = new("tx");
      tx.set_command(UVM_TLM_WRITE_COMMAND);
      tx.set_address($urandom_range(0, 'hFFFF));
      tx.set_data_length(4);
      tx.set_data(new[4]);
      delay.set_time_ps(20_000);  // Set 20 ns delay for each transaction

      // Perform the blocking transport
      socket.b_transport(tx, delay);

      // Update local time within the time quantum
      local_time += delay.get_realtime(1ns);

      // Check if we need to synchronize with global time
      if (local_time >= tqm.get_global_quantum()) begin
        tqm.sync();  // Synchronize with global time
        local_time = 0;  // Reset local time after synchronization
      end

      `uvm_info(get_type_name(), $sformatf("Transaction completed at local time %0t", local_time), UVM_LOW)
    end

    // Final synchronization to ensure all time is accounted for
    tqm.sync();

    phase.drop_objection(this);
  endtask
endclass

class time_quantum_manager;
  local time m_global_quantum;  // The maximum time a process can run ahead
  local time m_local_time;  // Tracks the local time within the current quantum

  function new(string name = "time_quantum_manager");
    m_global_quantum = 100ns;  // Default quantum
    m_local_time = 0;
  endfunction

  // Set the global time quantum
  function void set_global_quantum(time quantum);
    m_global_quantum = quantum;
  endfunction

  // Get the current global time quantum
  function time get_global_quantum();
    return m_global_quantum;
  endfunction

  // Synchronize local time with global simulation time

  // ===================================================================================== //
  // time current_time = $time;                                                            //
  // This line captures the current global simulation time using the SystemVerilog $time   //
  // system function. current_time now holds the current global simulation time.           //
  // ===================================================================================== //
  // if (m_local_time > current_time) begin                                                //
  // This condition checks if the local time (m_local_time) is ahead of the global         //
  // simulation time (current_time). This can happen because the process has been running  //
  // ahead within its time quantum without updating the global time.                       //
  // ===================================================================================== //
  // #(m_local_time - current_time);                                                       //
  // If the local time is ahead, this line advances the global simulation time.            //
  // The # operator in SystemVerilog is used to create a time delay.                       //
  // The delay is set to the difference between the local time and the current global time.//
  // This effectively "catches up" the global simulation time to match the local time.     //
  // ===================================================================================== //

  function void sync();
    time current_time = $time;
    if (m_local_time > current_time) begin
      #(m_local_time - current_time);  // Advance simulation time if needed
    end
    m_local_time = 0;  // Reset local time after synchronization
  endfunction

  // Increment local time within the current quantum
  function void incr_time(time t);
    m_local_time += t;
  endfunction
endclass
